      "tip": "Best eaten warm with butter. Order 'mit Butter' at a Bavarian bakery in the morning."
    },
    {
      "e": "🍺",
      "name": "Regional beer culture",
      "what": "Each region has its own style — Bavarian Helles (lager), Berliner Weisse (sour wheat), Cologne's Kölsch (served in tiny 200ml glasses)",
      "tip": "In Cologne, waiters refill your Kölsch automatically until you put a coaster on top of your glass."
    },
    {
      "e": "🥩",
      "name": "Schnitzel",
      "what": "Breaded and pan-fried veal or pork cutlet — best at a traditional Gasthaus (pub-restaurant)",
      "tip": "Authentic Wiener Schnitzel must be veal. Schnitzel Wiener Art (pork) is common and nearly as good."
    },
    {
      "e": "🧀",
      "name": "Käsespätzle",
      "what": "Swabian egg noodles baked with melted cheese and topped with fried onions — Germany's answer to mac and cheese",
      "tip": "A Baden-Württemberg staple. Rich and filling — one portion is usually enough for a full meal."
    },
    {
      "e": "🥐",
      "name": "Bäckerei (bakery) breakfast",
      "what": "Fresh bread rolls (Brötchen) with cold cuts, cheese, and butter — Germany's daily breakfast ritual",
      "tip": "Buy from a proper Bäckerei, not a supermarket. Germans are passionate about their bread — over 300 varieties."
    }
